  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How we know these names were popular",
      "content": "<p>Civil registration of births in England and Wales began in 1837, the year Victoria came to the throne, but the government did not publish lists of popular names until much later. The earliest official figures are the ONS top 100 for 1904, three years after Victoria died. Children named in 1904 were given names their parents' generation knew, so the list is a fair picture of late Victorian taste, though not of the 1840s.</p><p>For the earlier part of the century, Douglas Galbi's analysis of name samples shows how concentrated naming was: in 1800 Mary and John were each given to more than one child in five, and William overtook John as the top boys' name around 1840. By 1900 the ten commonest names covered far fewer children than in 1800, as parents spread across a wider range.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Revivals, royals and flowers",
      "content": "<p>Three fashions shaped Victorian naming. The first was the revival of Old English and medieval names, encouraged by novels and an interest in the national past: Alice, Edith, Hilda, Maud, Mabel and Victor all came back in the 1800s, and Arthur, used in England since the Middle Ages, had a surge in popularity. The second was royalty: Albert had been rare in England for two centuries until Prince Albert, Victoria's husband, made it popular again.</p><p>The third was flower and plant names. Violet, Ivy, Lily, Daisy and Rose were all in the 1904 top 50 for girls. Violet came into general English use and Daisy was first used as a name in the 1800s, and Rose, an old name, was revived with the flower in mind.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Using a Victorian name today",
      "content": "<p>Many of these names have come back. Florence, Ada, Alice, Arthur, Albert and Alfred are all in recent England and Wales rankings, while Gertrude, Mildred, Horace and Cyril are still rare. The ranking shown with each name is its recent position, so you can see which ones are popular again and which would still stand out.</p><p>For a period story, the 1904 note is the better guide: a name ranked 5th, like Arthur, would have been everywhere, while one near 100 would have been ordinary but not common.</p>"
    }
  ],
